 ## Overview
-Near-Far Linear Ray Generation generates a list of 3D sampling points along a camera ray segment defined by near ($t_{min}$) and far ($t_{max}$) distances for a specified number of samples [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of discretizing the continuous ray domain into a finite set of query points for volume rendering or neural radiance field evaluation. It sets itself apart from its siblings by providing the foundational uniform or disparity-based spacing logic upon which stratified, probabilistic, or depth-guided variants build [S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Inverse CDF Ray Sampling](11412-inverse-cdf-ray-sampling.md) which adapts density to learned probabilities, or [Depth-Guided Supersampling](12190-depth-guided-supersampling.md) which uses external depth priors, this method relies on fixed geometric intervals [S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
+Near-Far Linear Ray Generation generates a list of 3D sampling points along a camera ray segment defined by near ($t_{min}$) and far ($t_{max}$) distances for a specified number of samples [S1, S4, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by providing the foundational uniform or disparity-based spacing logic upon which stratified, probabilistic, or depth-guided variants build [S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Inverse CDF Ray Sampling](11412-inverse-cdf-ray-sampling.md) which adapts density to learned probabilities, or [Depth-Guided Supersampling](12190-depth-guided-supersampling.md) which uses external depth priors, this method relies on fixed geometric intervals [S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
